    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d310">

                <pb facs="0631" n="597" xml:id="pg597"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/11–852: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the United States
                        Mission at the United Nations</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d310-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-11-10T18:48:00-05:00">November 10, 1952—6:48
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Gadel 48. Re Korea, Delga 151, Delga 149.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d310-f2"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Confirming Gross–Hickerson–Johnson–Wainhouse–Henkin telecon, fol are
                        Dept’s comments on draft res Delga 151: <list>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Re para 2, insert words “for Korea” after words “armistice
                                agreement”.</item>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Re para 2B, Dept’s strong preference is for establishment
                                impartial comm to which GA wld name membs. US cld accept any state
                                outside Sov bloc. However, if necessary to obtain Ind agreement, US
                                wld accept provision for impartial comm leaving actual membership to
                                negots at Panmunjom. If Inds maintain that Chi Commies will accept
                                only membs Neutral Nations Supervisory Body (Sweden, Switzerland,
                                Poland, Czecho), plus “umpire”, US wld insist either that GA appoint
                                in advance “umpire” envisaged by Inds, or that, if 4 membs unable
                                agree upon umpire at first mtg, GA wld name “umpire” or possibly wld
                                delegate responsibility to appropriate internatl official such as
                                Pres ICJ. Preference in latter connection, however, wld be for GA
                                designate umpire in advance, his appointment to become effective if
                                4 states unable agree on “umpire” at first mtg. In discussing above
                                with Inds, GADel shld stress willingness accept impartial comm of
                                any 3 states outside Commie bloc, membs of UN or otherwise; if Inds
                                indicate Commies wld not accept such comm, US wld agree to 4
                                “neutral” states with GA naming umpire, as indicated above.</item>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Re para 2B(1). Substitute word “demilitarized” for
                                “neutral”.</item>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Re para 2B(3). Insert word “humanitarian” before word
                                “principles”.</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Fol are Dept’s tentative reactions to list of points developed by Menon
                        (Delga 149): <list>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Paras 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9, 10 acceptable.</item>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Paras 2 and 8 raise problem composition repatriation comm on which
                                Dept views already indicated in para 1<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>
                                above.</item>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>.</label>
                            <item>Dept considers para 7 unhappily worded and too detailed. Strongly
                                prefer formulation para 1 Delga 151.</item>
                            <label><hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>.</label>
                            <item>US cld not accept para 11. However, we can accept formulation on
                                polit conf set forth Delga 151 or provision according which GA
                                “approves” to holding of polit conf provided for in para 60 draft
                                armis agreement as indicated Gadel 41.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d310-f3"
                                /></item>
                        </list></item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bruce</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d310-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d310-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was drafted by Brown of UNP and cleared by Wainhouse and Johnson,
                    and with Hickerson.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d310-f2" n="2" xml:id="d310-f2-content">
                <p>Both dated Nov. 8, <ref target="#pg588">pp. 588</ref> and <ref target="#pg585"
                        >585</ref>, respectively.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d310-f3" n="3" xml:id="d310-f3-content">
                <p>In Gadel 41, dated Nov. 7, 1952, the Department of State gave its preliminary
                    comments on the list of elements basic to the U.S. position on Korea in the
                    General Assembly as set out in Delga 133, dated Nov. 6, <ref target="#pg579">p.
                        579</ref>, including acceptance of a political conference approved by the
                    General Assembly, if the Secretary of State concurred (320/11–652).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
